UPDATE: “Fake” Dan Sullivan VANISHES as FBI Agents Flood Alaska to Probe Peltola Campaign Election Scheme

Days before Alaska’s midterm Senate election, federal authorities have surged resources into the state to investigate an alleged Democratic effort to place a same-name candidate on the ballot to siphon votes from incumbent Republican Sen. Dan S. Sullivan.

The controversy centers on Daniel J. Sullivan Jr. (often called “Decoy Dan” or the “fake” Dan Sullivan), a retired teacher from the remote fishing town of Petersburg. He appears on the ballot as Daniel J. Sullivan Jr. with no party affiliation, next to the incumbent listed as Dan S. Sullivan (Republican, incumbent). Mary Peltola, the Democratic nominee and former U.S. representative, is the main challenger in a race that could help decide Senate control. Alaska uses ranked-choice voting.

A New York Times investigation detailed a “ballot composition project” in which Peltola campaign aides and Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee operatives allegedly searched for other Dan Sullivans, recruited candidates (including this one) via a Midwestern operative using a false name, and worked to dilute the incumbent’s support. Peltola has denied any knowledge or involvement, calling the claims offensive and stating she has no interest in tricking voters. The DSCC and others have also denied recruiting him. Daniel J. Sullivan has previously said he is running independently.

Alaska election officials tried to remove him from the primary ballot over intent to confuse voters. Courts, including the state Supreme Court, ordered him restored, finding no legal “good faith” requirement for candidacy, though they allowed clearer ballot distinctions.

U.S. Attorney Michael Heyman confirmed a months-long criminal probe that accelerated after the Times report, with a “significant surge” of FBI agents and attorneys. Agents have interviewed donors (including in Petersburg), asking whether people were misled about which Sullivan they supported. The DOJ has issued dozens of subpoenas and is seeking a court order to isolate ballots ranking Daniel J. Sullivan to check for confusion. Gov. Mike Dunleavy ordered a parallel state investigation. President Trump has called for prosecutions and ballot removal.

Reporters describe the Petersburg candidate as increasingly difficult to reach. He has not responded to recent inquiries, locals and news crews have struggled to locate him, and reports say he left town ahead of the election—fueling the “vanishes” framing in some coverage. No charges have been filed, and authorities have not publicly named targets. The election is imminent, with both civil and criminal tracks still active.

